1. No. It is impossible to construct a sequence where two versions of the same class code 3 container are both readable and reachable. The published predicate I-1.2 states b <= T_pub for publication, but during rebuild, the system traverses from the final published root. Any intermediate container version not referenced by the final root is unreachable regardless of its key. For example: a checkpoint txg=10 with transaction number 100 allocated. Fixed point round 1 writes container X to block A with key (10,5,100). Round 2 writes container X to block B with key (10,5,100). The final root points to block B. Block A is unreferenced and thus ignored during rebuild. No two versions of the same container can be reachable with identical keys because only the version referenced by the root is traversed. The tie-breaker premise in clause 1 is false since stale versions are never reachable.

2. Arm C does not break. With clustered allocation (D3 settled item 5), all metadata blocks are pre-allocated in a segment before writing. The fixed point converges in memory, then all data is written in one go. A crash mid-publication leaves the checkpoint unpublished (T_pub not updated), so recovery uses the previous checkpoint. The 2 GiB dirty data limit is handled by pre-allocating all required blocks in the clustered segment before writing. No allocation during write avoids further iterations. The system enforces a check that no container is written multiple times per checkpoint, satisfying the project rule against reminder sentences.

3. Yes. A reader that does not check class code and assumes the transaction number field always represents a transaction counter (per D23) misreads code 3 containers. For example, a code 3 container written in fixed point round 2 has transaction number field=2 (round ordinal under Arm A). A generic reader treating this as a transaction counter would incorrectly group all code 3 containers with transaction number=2 as part of the same transaction. However, code 3 containers are not transaction records; D23 specifies transaction numbers apply only to user transaction records. This causes incorrect transaction grouping and data corruption during rebuild or recovery.

4. Yes. The rebuild process only follows pointers from the final published root (T_pub). Intermediate container versions from fixed point rounds are not referenced by the final root. Invariant I-1.2 ensures b <= T_pub for publication, but the rebuild scan starts from the root and traverses only reachable blocks. Unreferenced intermediate versions are garbage and never included in the rebuild. This makes the tie-breaker unnecessary as only the referenced version is used, regardless of key similarity.
